1
00:00:03,800 --> 00:00:11,088
In 2024, Labour won nearly two thirds of
the seats in the House of Commons, with
about a third of the vote.

2
00:00:11,508 --> 00:00:15,066
Reform UK won one vote in seven, and five
seats.

3
00:00:15,066 --> 00:00:18,770
The Greens won one vote in fifteen, and
four seats.

4
00:00:19,190 --> 00:00:21,083
That's first past the post.

5
00:00:21,083 --> 00:00:27,575
Each seat goes to whoever tops one local
count, and every other vote in that seat
elects nobody.

6
00:00:27,995 --> 00:00:37,685
This is a proposal that keeps the simple
cross on the ballot paper, keeps MPs tied
to a place, and gives parties seats in
proportion to their votes.

7
00:00:37,685 --> 00:00:40,351
It's called proportional approval voting.

8
00:00:41,351 --> 00:00:43,007
Start with the map.

9
00:00:43,007 --> 00:00:49,135
Take today's 650 constituencies, and
combine them in groups of about five.

10
00:00:49,555 --> 00:00:55,537
That makes roughly 125 to 130 districts,
each electing about five MPs.

11
00:00:55,537 --> 00:01:02,277
The Commons stays at 650 seats, and no
boundaries have to be drawn from scratch.

12
00:01:03,077 --> 00:01:05,027
The ballot looks like this.

13
00:01:05,027 --> 00:01:11,506
Candidates are grouped by party, and you
put a cross next to every candidate you
approve of.

14
00:01:11,506 --> 00:01:12,899
As many as you like.

15
00:01:13,319 --> 00:01:15,761
Each party also has a box of its own.

16
00:01:15,761 --> 00:01:22,828
A cross there approves all of that party's
candidates, so voting a straight party
ticket still takes one mark.

17
00:01:23,248 --> 00:01:24,859
And you can't spoil it.

18
00:01:24,859 --> 00:01:31,572
Tick a party's box and some of its
candidates as well, and the extra crosses
simply change nothing.

19
00:01:31,572 --> 00:01:34,324
Every pattern of crosses is a valid vote.

20
00:01:35,224 --> 00:01:38,075
The count fills the seats one at a time.

21
00:01:38,075 --> 00:01:44,889
Every ballot starts at full strength, and
the candidate with the most support wins
the first seat.

22
00:01:45,309 --> 00:01:50,583
After that, every ballot that approved the
winner counts for a third as much.

23
00:01:50,583 --> 00:01:54,235
Once a second candidate it approved has
won, a fifth.

24
00:01:54,235 --> 00:01:55,993
Then a seventh, and so on.

25
00:01:56,413 --> 00:02:00,728
Here's a five-seat district with a hundred
voters.

26
00:02:00,728 --> 00:02:07,411
Forty-two back party A, thirty-one party
B, seventeen party C, and ten party D.

27
00:02:07,831 --> 00:02:08,832
Round one.

28
00:02:08,832 --> 00:02:12,565
A's first candidate wins with forty-two.

29
00:02:12,565 --> 00:02:16,024
A's voters now count a third as much:

30
00:02:16,024 --> 00:02:16,843
fourteen.

31
00:02:17,263 --> 00:02:23,794
Round two goes to B, with thirty-one, and
B's voters drop to just over ten.

32
00:02:23,794 --> 00:02:27,059
Round three goes to C, with seventeen.

33
00:02:27,479 --> 00:02:28,427
Round four.

34
00:02:28,427 --> 00:02:33,089
A's fourteen is now the highest, so A
takes a second seat.

35
00:02:33,089 --> 00:02:34,037
Round five.

36
00:02:34,037 --> 00:02:37,040
B's ten point three edges out D's ten.

37
00:02:37,460 --> 00:02:40,328
Two seats for A, two for B, one for C.

38
00:02:40,328 --> 00:02:45,401
Close to their shares of the vote, and
nobody's ballot counted twice.

39
00:02:45,821 --> 00:02:55,956
Those divisors, one, three, five, seven,
are the Sainte-Laguë method, which New
Zealand and Germany use to share out seats
between parties.

40
00:02:55,956 --> 00:03:01,965
The difference here is that voters, not
parties, decide which candidates fill
them.

41
00:03:02,965 --> 00:03:04,591
So what would it do?

42
00:03:04,591 --> 00:03:13,727
Take the 2024 results, swing them to
YouGov's poll of the eighth and ninth of
November 2026, and count them both ways.

43
00:03:14,147 --> 00:03:21,719
Under first past the post, Labour would
win 287 seats on 27 percent of the vote.

44
00:03:21,719 --> 00:03:25,458
Under proportional approval voting, 189.

45
00:03:25,878 --> 00:03:32,863
The Gallagher index, the standard measure
of how far seats stray from votes, falls
from 15.

46
00:03:32,863 --> 00:03:33,401
2 to 3.

47
00:03:33,401 --> 00:03:33,631
8.

48
00:03:33,631 --> 00:03:35,857
That's about Ireland's level.

49
00:03:36,277 --> 00:03:38,804
And the choice of divisors matters.

50
00:03:38,804 --> 00:03:46,945
With the older weights of a half, a third,
a quarter, which give the D'Hondt method,
the Greens would win 32 seats.

51
00:03:46,945 --> 00:03:49,051
With Sainte-Laguë weights, 59.

52
00:03:50,051 --> 00:03:51,664
Why not something simpler?

53
00:03:51,664 --> 00:03:55,667
Keep single-member seats, and just use
approval voting in each one.

54
00:03:56,087 --> 00:04:02,344
It would elect better local MPs, because
broadly acceptable candidates would win
more often.

55
00:04:02,344 --> 00:04:09,880
But it isn't proportional, and the
electoral reform movement judges every
proposal by whether seats match votes.

56
00:04:10,300 --> 00:04:20,200
And with Reform UK polling between a fifth
and a third of the vote, and many voters
firmly opposed to them, approval in single
seats would look like a scheme to lock
them out.

57
00:04:21,100 --> 00:04:24,771
The main rival is the single transferable
vote.

58
00:04:24,771 --> 00:04:28,747
Like this proposal, it uses multi-member
districts.

59
00:04:28,747 --> 00:04:34,788
Ireland uses it, and so do Scotland's
councils and Northern Ireland's Assembly.

60
00:04:35,208 --> 00:04:38,498
STV asks voters to rank the candidates.

61
00:04:38,498 --> 00:04:43,515
Proportional approval keeps the same cross
people make today.

62
00:04:43,935 --> 00:04:46,132
STV can fail monotonicity:

63
00:04:46,132 --> 00:04:50,202
a candidate can lose because they gained
support.

64
00:04:50,202 --> 00:04:54,515
Under approval, more support never hurts a
candidate.

65
00:04:54,935 --> 00:05:00,704
STV sometimes pushes voters to rank a more
electable candidate above their real
favourite.

66
00:05:00,704 --> 00:05:04,444
Approval never asks you to put anyone
above your favourite.

67
00:05:04,864 --> 00:05:07,830
And the count is dramatically simpler.

68
00:05:07,830 --> 00:05:21,673
STV sets a quota, passes winners' surplus
votes on at fractional values, eliminates
the last-placed candidate, transfers those
ballots, and repeats, recalculating round
after round.

69
00:05:21,673 --> 00:05:24,639
Scotland's councils run it by computer.

70
00:05:25,059 --> 00:05:27,661
Proportional approval has one rule.

71
00:05:27,661 --> 00:05:38,215
Add up each candidate's approvals, with
every ballot weighted one, a third, or a
fifth, depending on how many of its
candidates have already won.

72
00:05:38,215 --> 00:05:40,889
You could check it with a calculator.

73
00:05:41,309 --> 00:05:44,771
STV's real advantage is a longer track
record.

74
00:05:44,771 --> 00:05:52,725
Proportional approval's comes from Sweden,
which used it to allocate seats within
parties from 1909 to 1921.

75
00:05:53,625 --> 00:05:59,346
Why not closed party lists, like the ones
Wales now uses for the Senedd?

76
00:05:59,346 --> 00:06:05,380
Under closed lists, the party decides
which of its candidates get its seats.

77
00:06:05,380 --> 00:06:09,769
Here, the voters decide, and they can
cross party lines.

78
00:06:10,189 --> 00:06:14,956
That also answers the strongest argument
for the additional member system:

79
00:06:14,956 --> 00:06:18,261
that voters should have a personal link to
their MP.

80
00:06:19,061 --> 00:06:26,914
And the additional member system itself,
used for the Scottish Parliament and
Germany's Bundestag, creates two classes
of MP:

81
00:06:26,914 --> 00:06:31,339
constituency members, and list members who
owe their seat to the party.

82
00:06:31,759 --> 00:06:33,443
It also has overhang.

83
00:06:33,443 --> 00:06:42,704
When a party wins more constituencies than
its vote entitles it to, you either add
seats, or refuse to seat some winners.

84
00:06:43,124 --> 00:06:48,209
Germany's Bundestag grew to 736 members in
2021.

85
00:06:48,209 --> 00:06:54,748
After the fix, 23 constituency winners
were not seated in 2025.

86
00:06:55,168 --> 00:07:01,148
Even with approval voting for the
constituency seats, the list tier would
cancel it out.

87
00:07:01,148 --> 00:07:09,144
Approval helps consensus candidates win
locally, and then the top-up seats
subtract those wins from each party's
total.

88
00:07:09,564 --> 00:07:16,277
And separate tiers invite decoy lists, as
parties have used in Albania, Lesotho and
Venezuela.

89
00:07:17,277 --> 00:07:22,937
There's one thing proportional approval
does that no other system offers.

90
00:07:22,937 --> 00:07:25,767
Voters decide how proportional it is.

91
00:07:26,187 --> 00:07:31,829
If you approve only your own party's
candidates, you get the proportionality of
a list system.

92
00:07:32,249 --> 00:07:38,432
If you also approve a broadly acceptable
candidate, you spend some of your side's
weight on that seat.

93
00:07:38,432 --> 00:07:44,736
That support can push the consensus
candidate over the line, ahead of the
opposing side's next candidate.

94
00:07:45,156 --> 00:07:52,810
In the simulation, cross-party approval
cuts Reform UK from 140 seats to 120.

95
00:07:53,230 --> 00:07:57,569
Under STV, ranking someone lower down
costs you nothing.

96
00:07:57,569 --> 00:08:00,309
Which also means it changes nothing.

97
00:08:01,309 --> 00:08:05,907
There are real costs, and they should be
stated up front.

98
00:08:06,327 --> 00:08:07,742
First, a threshold.

99
00:08:07,742 --> 00:08:14,036
In a five-seat district, a party needs
roughly ten to fifteen percent of the
local vote.

100
00:08:14,036 --> 00:08:21,321
Restore Britain, on three percent, would
win no seats, and the Greens would miss
out in many districts.

101
00:08:21,741 --> 00:08:23,400
Second, free-riding.

102
00:08:23,400 --> 00:08:27,822
Voters may skip a candidate they think is
already safe.

103
00:08:27,822 --> 00:08:31,850
Every proportional system has some version
of this.

104
00:08:32,270 --> 00:08:33,537
Third, the count.

105
00:08:33,537 --> 00:08:41,209
Results can't be added up polling station
by polling station, so each district
counts its ballots centrally.

106
00:08:41,209 --> 00:08:44,939
Scotland already does this for its council
elections.

107
00:08:45,359 --> 00:08:54,815
And it has never elected a national
parliament, though Sweden has used methods
from this family to allocate seats within
parties since 1909.

108
00:08:55,815 --> 00:08:57,573
So the ballot is a cross.

109
00:08:57,573 --> 00:08:59,127
The count is one rule.

110
00:08:59,127 --> 00:09:03,521
And the Commons ends up looking like the
country that elected it.

111
00:09:03,941 --> 00:09:06,137
Proportional approval voting.

112
00:09:06,137 --> 00:09:09,871
Approve as many as you like, and every
vote counts.

